# Mutation plan for the thirteen reads in `mm_api::gated_reads` (eleven api4 files, one module).
#
#   scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/gated-reads.plan
#
# Every route refuses, so mutating "does it refuse" proves nothing. What is worth mutating is
# **which** refusal, **what runs first**, and **whether a session is demanded at all** — the three
# things a reader could plausibly get wrong by copying a neighbour in the same module.
#
# The three config-sourced gates get their own lines because each is the whole content of its
# route on a stock server, and each defaults to a value that makes the route refuse: flipping the
# default turns a 501 into a forward.
#
# **Three mutations were re-pointed after a first run reported them SURVIVED**, and none was a gap
# in the code:
#
#   * the two config defaults belong to the *config* oracle, not to a route suite. The live
#     document supplies both values explicitly, so no request can tell the default apart from the
#     document — `every_default_matches_what_go_actually_wrote` is what compares them, and it only
#     started covering these two once `dump-config-fixture.sh` stopped projecting six sections.
#   * `session_has_permission_to_and_not_restricted_admin` differs from the plain check **only**
#     when `ExperimentalSettings.RestrictSystemAdmin` is true, and it is false on this deployment
#     with no route to change it. Mutating the handler's *choice* of check is therefore invisible
#     over HTTP; the mutation moved to the app function, where `db_authorization_by_post` plants
#     the setting and can see it. That the support-packet handler picks the restricted variant is
#     recorded in its doc comment, which is where someone changing the line will read it.
#
# The last two lines are the no-op controls and must SURVIVE.

